<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    A caller with six parameters loses the argument it tests.

        int six(int a, int i, int b, int c, int d, int s) {
          unsigned char buf[16]; buf[0] = a; unsigned char *p = buf;
          e(s); if (s & 1) p++;
          p[i & 7] = b + c + d;
          if (buf[0]) return ext(buf[0]) + 1;
          return 5;
        }

    clang 22 -O2 (-fno-stack-protector -fcf-protection=none, linked
    -nostdlib -static -Ttext=0x401000, ext and e defined in the image) loads
    buf[0] into edi, tests it there and calls ext, which reads edi.
    onlyOpUse refuses the trial (the value also feeds the CBRANCH), and
    calleereadarg declined to restore it because six's own entry reads r9,
    the last SysV argument register, which it took as a sign that six may
    be variadic.  six reads r9 with mov %r9d,%ebx: a sixth parameter, not a
    register-save prologue, which only stores the registers.  kuna printed
    `return ext() + 1;`.

    Stage: P4 active input-trial scoring (kuna_calleereadarg.rs, always on).
    The caller counts as possibly variadic only when its entry reads the
    last argument register just to store it.
-->
